@*
    Services - what the app can do.

    THIS SCREEN EXISTS BECAUSE NOBODY COULD FIND OUT. The repository carries 165
    projects across 43 life domains and the app surfaced one of them. Somebody with
    the whole source tree open could not have said it does agriculture; a person
    holding the phone had no chance.

    NOTHING SCROLLS. Seven sections of tiles stacked would be a scroll nobody
    finishes, which is the same failure as hiding them - a capability below the
    fold is a capability that does not exist. So the sections are chips and one
    section's tiles show at a time: eight tiles, two rows, always whole on the
    screen.

    A tile OPENS THE CONVERSATION pointed at the thing, rather than going to a
    screen. That is what makes breadth free - thirty-nine tiles need thirty-nine
    first sentences, not thirty-nine screens - and it is how an assistant works:
    you ask it, you do not navigate to it.
*@
@page "/services"
@inject NavigationManager Nav

<div class="services">

    <div class="chips" role="tablist">
        @for (var i = 0; i < Capabilities.All.Count; i++)
        {
            var index = i;
            <button class="chip @(i == _group ? "chip-on" : null)"
                    role="tab" @onclick="() => _group = index">
                @Capabilities.All[i].Title
            </button>
        }
    </div>

    <div class="grid">
        @foreach (var c in Capabilities.All[_group].Items)
        {
            <button class="tile" @onclick="() => Open(c)">
                <span class="tile-icon" aria-hidden="true">@c.Icon</span>
                <span class="tile-label">@c.Title</span>
            </button>
        }
    </div>

</div>

@code {
    int _group;

    /// <summary>
    /// Open a capability: its own screen where one genuinely exists, otherwise the
    /// conversation with the first sentence already written.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// MOST OF THESE HAVE NO SCREEN AND SHOULD NOT. Forty screens for forty
    /// capabilities is how an assistant becomes a menu system. The two that go
    /// somewhere - the CV and the language list - are a form and a list rather
    /// than a question.
    /// <para>
    /// The opener is put in the box rather than sent, so somebody nudged into a
    /// subject can change it first.
    /// </para>
    /// </remarks>
    void Open(Capability c)
        => Nav.NavigateTo(c.Route ?? $"chat?ask={Uri.EscapeDataString(c.Opener)}");
}
